A developer attempts to optimize memory in a .NET 10 asynchronous method by writing the following code:

public async Task<int> ProcessPayloadAsync(string jsonPayload) {
    ReadOnlySpan<char> span = jsonPayload.AsSpan();
    int id = FastParseId(span);

    await Task.Delay(100);

    int status = FastParseStatus(span);
    return id + status;
}

The code fails to compile.
MANDATORY REQUIREMENTS:
1. Explain the exact technical reason why a 'ref struct' (such as Span<T>) cannot be part of an 'async' method state machine.
2. Provide a refactoring that DOES COMPILE in C#: process Spans in synchronous helper methods (non-async) BEFORE or AFTER the 'await', or use 'ReadOnlyMemory<char>'.

